WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.638
A greedy algorithm commits to the best immediate option at each step without backtracking.

00:00:06.638 --> 00:00:11.948
Unlike dynamic programming, it explores only a single path of decisions.

00:00:11.948 --> 00:00:17.480
Because choices are permanent, the ordering criterion must be proven sound.

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:24.855
In classic interval scheduling, sorting tasks by earliest finish time leaves the maximum room for future intervals.

00:00:24.855 --> 00:00:28.738
Always pick the next compatible interval that finishes soonest.

00:00:28.738 --> 00:00:33.607
Sorting by earliest start or shortest duration fails on simple counterexamples.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:39.209
Justify a greedy rule with an exchange argument before trusting it in a contest.

00:00:39.209 --> 00:00:47.240
Compare a hypothetical optimal schedule against your greedy choice and show that swapping them never hurts the objective.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:51.620
Test small random inputs against brute force to catch flaws early.

00:00:51.917 --> 00:00:58.199
Some greedy problems require dynamic re-ordering or regret adjustments as new items arrive.

00:00:58.199 --> 00:01:05.655
Maintain chosen items in a min-heap or max-heap so you can replace the worst commitment in logarithmic time.

00:01:05.655 --> 00:01:09.728
Regret greedy combines local speed with global flexibility.
